  </table>
  <p>
    Four of the twenty-four have pages of their own here and twenty do not, which is a decision
    rather than an unfinished job: a holder earns a node when something about that holder does not
    fit the line. Samuel A. Baxter was mayor twice and appointed once between, and this site
    used to say he was probably the Dr. Samuel A. Baxter who co-wrote the 1906 county history. The
    1906 book's own list of mayors calls the man of 1851 "Samuel A. Baxter, Sr." and the man of
    1896 "Dr. Samuel A. Baxter", and a list that says Senior is distinguishing him from someone.
    Father and son fits the printed evidence better than one man of eighty-one, and the
    identification is now unresolved rather than probable. George W. Overmyer is the only
    mayor in the line no election produced; the council chose him in September 1869 when Robb
    resigned, and he was probate judge in 1881 on the committee that picked the plans for the
    present courthouse. Richard Metheany appears in the table six times across twenty-six years.
    And two sources spell H. S. Prophet differently.
  </p>
  <h2>Three books, four slots, and a year with nobody in it</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={lostYear} />
  <p>
    The 1906 and 1921 histories print this same line, and all three agree on every one of forty-odd
    names except four consecutive slots in the 1850s. The 1885 line reads Milligan, Baxter,
    Milligan, Baxter. The 1906 list drops the second Baxter. The 1921 list drops the second
    Milligan and the second Baxter both — which is what a copyist does reading a repeated pair.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which of them is right is settled without leaving the page. Delete Baxter's second term and
    the two names after it slide two years earlier, until James A. Newell's April 1858 stops them,
    leaving April 1857 to April 1858 with no mayor at all — the only such hole in the 1906 list's
    thirty-four entries. A list that merely disagreed would not leave a year empty.
  </p>
  <p>
    Collating the three also found an error here rather than in them. The 1885 mayor list prints
    "John B. Townsend"; the same volume prints James B. Townsend in its election returns, in its
    index, and in a biography of the man — an attorney born in 1851 who paid for his legal studies
    by touring the country with a company of vocalists selling his father's patent medicines, and
    who took the office at twenty-five. Two other corrections that looked identical were not taken:
    the 1885 book prints Delzell seventeen times against Dalzell four, and the 1906 and 1921 lists
    are one lineage rather than two witnesses, since the 1921 book says it follows "the most recent
    report in Lima."
  </p>

  <h2>The line to 1922</h2>
  <p>
    The office did not run out; it was abolished. The commission-manager form of government came
    into effect on 1 January 1922, at the end of Mayor Burkhardt's term, after what the 1921
    history calls four-score years in which Lima "has been ruled by a mayor" — the town organized
    in March 1842, so four-score is exact to the quarter.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Mayors of Lima, 1886–1922</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Years</th><th scope="col">Mayor</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1884–90</td><td>William McComb, Jr.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1890–92</td><td>Ira R. Longsworth</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1892–94</td><td>William McComb, Jr.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1894–96</td><td>James V. Smiley</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1896–98</td><td>Dr. Samuel A. Baxter</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1898–1900</td><td>H. S. Prophet</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1900–02</td><td>William McComb, Jr.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1902–04</td><td>L. H. Rogers</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1904–06</td><td>Theodore D. Robb</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1906–08</td><td>Theodore D. Robb</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1908–10</td><td>Fred C. Becker</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1910–12</td><td>George Dyer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1912–14</td><td>C. N. Shook</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1914–16</td><td>Theodore D. Robb, who died in office</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1916–18</td><td>B. H. Simpson</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1918–20</td><td>B. H. Simpson</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1920–22</td><td>F. A. Burkhardt</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Everything to 1904 is dated by the 1906 history. Everything after it is dated here: the 1921
    list gives nine entries after L. H. Rogers and no years at all, but it states the term — two
    years — and it dates the abolition. Nine two-year terms from April 1904 land in April 1922,
    and the quarter-year residue is the calendar moving, which the same 1906 book shows in its
    list of city solicitors, where every boundary is April until one turns on January 1906.
  </p>
  <p>
    The book checks itself once and passes: Theodore D. Robb appears three times in the list, and
    a biographical passage elsewhere in the same volume calls him "three times mayor of Lima". He
    died in the third, and the list says the term "was finished by Miles Standish", who is named
    nowhere else in it. The last mayor, F. A. Burkhardt, sat on the book's own advisory board of
    editors and wrote a chapter in it — so the volume's enthusiasm for the charter that ended his
    office was printed while he held it.
  </p>
  <p>
    What is still missing is ninety-nine years, 1922 to 2021, and not for want of asking: the
    city's site names its sitting mayor, dates her first election to 2021, and names no
    predecessor. Its sitemap carries no roster of former mayors, and its only history page belongs
    to the fire department.
  </p>

  <p>
    The book prints the whole council beside the mayor, and that is where the rest of this site
    turns up. Benjamin C. Faurot was elected a village trustee in 1867, eighteen years before the
    well that made him. Matthias H. Nichols was a trustee in 1849, after three terms in Congress
    and twelve years before he raised the county's first company for the war. Thomas K. Jacobs was
    trustee, marshal and treasurer, and introduced the bill in the Ohio House in April 1861 to tax
    the county for soldiers' families.
  </p>
</Article>
